 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Transvoxel Smooth Terrain Texturing](7280-transvoxel-smooth-terrain-texturing.md)
-This method applies triplanar texturing to surfaces extracted by the Transvoxel algorithm for terrain rendering, blending "top" and "side" textures (or a material array) using a "topness" factor derived from the surface normal. It builds on [Transvoxel](6277-transvoxel.md) for geometry generation.
+This method applies procedural and texture-based color blending to surfaces extracted by the Transvoxel algorithm, solving the visual discontinuity of flat shading on dynamically changing isosurfaces. It differs from standard single-texture approaches by using triplanar mapping to avoid UV seams and blending between top/side textures based on surface normal orientation. It also supports multi-material blending via texture arrays and vertex weights for complex terrain features like grass, stone, and forest patches.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of voxels processed, but with a higher constant factor due to neighbor lookups and transition cell handling [S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the voxel grid and transition cell lookup tables. The lookup tables are pre-computed and static [S2, unofficial].
-- **Measured Performance:** Performance depends on the GPU's ability to handle the geometry shader and texture lookups. The method is efficient for large terrains with varying LODs [S2, unofficial].
-- **Scaling:** Scales well with LOD, as higher LODs process fewer voxels. The transition handling adds overhead only at LOD boundaries [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Produces watertight meshes across LOD boundaries; integrates well with GPU geometry shaders; suitable for terrain rendering [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Requires pre-computed transition cell data; complex implementation compared to standard Marching Cubes; potential for visual artifacts if projection is not enabled [S2, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** When rendering large voxel-based terrains or volumes with varying levels of detail [S1][S2, unofficial].
-- **When not to use:** For uniform grids where standard Marching Cubes suffices; for scenes requiring dynamic LOD changes without pre-computed transition data [S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of voxels, but with a higher constant factor due to neighbor lookups and transition cell processing.
+- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the volume data plus lookup tables for transition cells. The lookup tables are relatively small but must be pre-computed.
+- **Performance:** Efficient on GPU due to parallel voxel processing. The geometry shader implementation allows for on-the-fly mesh generation without CPU intervention [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Handles dynamic LOD changes seamlessly; maintains watertight surfaces; no manual mesh stitching required.
+- **Limitations:** Requires pre-computed transition cell data; complex implementation due to multiple cell types; potential for artifacts if LOD function is not smooth.
+- **When to use:** Dynamic terrain rendering, large-scale environments with varying detail levels.
+- **When not to use:** Static scenes with uniform resolution; scenes requiring extremely high precision at boundaries.
 
 ## Background
-The Transvoxel algorithm was introduced as a solution to the LOD problem in voxel-based rendering, specifically to connect meshes of different LODs without gaps [S1]. It extends the Marching Cubes technique by introducing a new set of polygon configurations for transition cells [S1]. The method has been implemented in various contexts, including WebGL for volumetric terrain rendering [S1] and as a geometry shader implementation for OpenGL [S2, unofficial].
+The Transvoxel algorithm was introduced as a solution to the Level-of-Detail (LOD) problem in volumetric rendering [S1]. It extends the Marching Cubes technique by adding specific polygon configurations for transition cells [S1]. The method is particularly relevant for GPU-based extraction, where geometry shaders can handle the complex logic of transitioning between different voxel resolutions [S2, unofficial]. It is widely used in terrain rendering and large-scale scientific visualization.
 
